hanges (no embedding model, a refused credential or width). The retrievable filter's `msg:` branch decides by the message's conversation (`conversationAssignmentAllows`, a live lifecycle, not spam), and only for a door that asks (`includeConversationMessages`: the chat tools, which cite a body hit as a `conversation` row and `rag_fetch` its whole text wrapped as mail; the SQL pre-filter excludes message rows for every other door, the sandbox bridge and the MCP door included, and searches them as a partition with its own candidate pool, so bodies a member may not read never push a document out of the page). Outbound mail and API-mirrored conversations are not indexed. Mail stored before this change is not backfilled; a mailbox connected after it indexes every inbound email its first sync imports, bounded by the queue's batch and the organization's embedding limiter. Release: liveness is the inbound message row of a conversation not marked spam (`assessMessageRefLiveness`); `deleteConversation` and the retention window queue `knowledge.release_refs` in the transaction that deletes the conversations, a spam verdict queues it and lifting the verdict queues the index again (`domains/conversations/message-corpus.ts`), and the corpus reconcile walks message refs apart from blob refs, from a random pivot. Real-Postgres proof: the `checkInboundEmailBodies` lane (`domains/knowledge/message-index.integration.ts`). Emailed ATTACHMENTS are mail too: 0.4's `indexFileBlob` stamped an attachment's corpus row with its conversation and the 0.5 port dropped it, so the pre-filter read the row as an org-wide hub row and the chat labelled and served it as a plain document. `indexUploadedFile` stamps it again (`emailedAttachmentConversation`: an unbound file row's conversation, unless an active document holds its ref, which it then indexes as, under that document's scope — the ref's holder, the lowest-id active document when several share the ref as WebDAV COPY twins and two documents bound from one upload do, which a file filed into another twin indexes as too and whose scope every scope writer writes), the nightly corpus reconcile backfills rows indexed before (`reconcileMailAttachmentStamps`, which walks every attachment each night and also releases the corpus copy of one whose conversation was deleted or marked spam before its lane queued a release; a second walk over the stamped corpus rows takes the stamp off a row no attachment backs any more — filed into or held by a document, or a thread/chat file — reading the backing again after the clear so a ref that turned back into an attachment meanwhile gets its stamp back (one of a deleted or spam conversation before its corpus release), or releases it when nothing keeps it in the corpus, its bytes and a trashed file row that only remembers them included when nothing references them, and a byte delete that fails there or in the reconcile's blob walk re-queues `knowledge.release_refs`, in jobs of at most 500 refs as the mail lanes and a task retire queue theirs (`queueRefRelease`), its log line saying whether it was re-queued; each clear locks at most 100 corpus rows in a transaction of its own, its recheck read under a statement timeout on an app connection taken before the lock and under a 15 s bound of its own for a connection that stops answering during the clear, which then rolls back and the pass goes on without that connection, and one that cannot finish rolls back for the next night; the log keeps the two walks' counts apart, and the recheck's restamps and releases apart from the clears; the scope pass leaves conversation stamps alone, so its drift count stays scope writes that failed or never ran — a per-edit sync the corpus refused, an edit whose sync writes less than the whole scope (a WebDAV folder MOVE into a team folder re-stamps the paths alone), or a change of a shared ref's holder on a lane that does not re-stamp it — and it and the per-edit syncs write a shared ref from its holder alone, so an edit of either twin is not drift the next night; a write that moves a shared ref's holder without editing a scope — a WebDAV COPY, trash (a folder cascade and an overwritten destination included) or PUT overwrite, a governance Trash restore, any hard delete (`purgeDocument`), a sync update's or an entry delete's old ref — re-stamps the ref from its new holder after its commit (`syncRagRefHolderScopes`); the first night after the release that makes every scope writer write the holder (#3808) the pass corrects, once, every row an older release stamped from a copy of the ref rather than its holder, a burst of drift that is no failed sync), and the retrievable filter decides an attachment exactly like a body (`decideMailRetrievable`, which also refuses a scopeless caller); bodies and attachments are one `mail` partition that only the chat tools ask for, the corpus pre-filter keeps every conversation-stamped row out of every other door, the chat wraps an attachment as untrusted mail (provenance from the file rows while a row is unstamped), mail rows never take part in content-hash clones, and corpus-liveness reads an attachment of a deleted or spam conversation as dead while its bytes stay — so delete and spam release its corpus copy, lifting spam re-queues `rag.index_file`, and the retention window releases it with the conversation. Real-Postgres proof: the `checkEmailedAttachments` lane (`domains/knowledge/attachment-mail.integration.ts`). |
