./services/platform/tests/manual/reference/pins.md:40:| `KNOW-F18` | Whether the Alert stands over the page list was decided from the rows the list held: a failed or skipped row in view said why, so the Alert stayed away. The list was a 20-row window, so the same scan's Alert came and went with **Load more**, and once KNOW-F36 narrowed it to one state it came and went with the segment too (shown over twenty indexed rows, hidden once a failed one loaded, shown again under an empty **Skipped**); an empty segment on a row that had not yet counted its pages read as a hollow scan and took the list with it (#4068, 2026-10-02). The site's counts decide now — those every pages answer carries, the row's failed count while the first read is out, none once it failed — in `hasFailedOrSkippedPages`. They hide only the one reason the rows explain, the crawl action's tally (`isPageTallyReason`): the first repair let any failed or skipped page hide every reason, so a lost database or a sitemap timeout showed nowhere (review of #4135). `scan-error.test.ts` and `website-view-dialog.test.tsx`, which walks every segment and window, hold it. |
./services/platform/tests/manual/reference/pins.md:99:| `TASK-F18` / `TASK-F52` | The page used to intersect its board read with the palette's search, whose first 25 hits ignore the facets: past 25 matches a task the facets kept could fall outside them and every lane read **No tasks** (#3745), and a board that included archived tasks had to hope the search did too (#3325). The search is now a board filter — the doors take `q` and match it in the statement that carries the board's cap (`service.board-search.test.ts`, `checkTaskBoardSearch`) — so F18 no longer judges two reads agreeing, and F51's Network check proves no capped search is asked. |
